From: Stel777 <274792865+Stel777@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 25 Jul 2026 20:14:01 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] feat(nav): put the real vault picker in the sidebar, with
 list/grid
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The sidebar switched vaults through a native <select>. Its option list is
drawn by the OS from `color-scheme`, which the theme sets on :root from the
app appearance — but the sidebar is dark in every theme, so under a light
appearance the OS painted a white list while the options inherited near-white
nav text. White on white, unfixable by changing appearance. It also gave no
way to browse 30+ vaults: no sort, no grid, one long native list.

Replace it with BrainSelector, which draws its own list and brings the sort
control and the list/grid toggle with it. Three small additions make it fit:

- `tone="nav"` so the TRIGGER uses the --nv-nav-* palette (app-theme text is
  unreadable on the always-dark sidebar). The dropdown keeps the app palette;
  it is a floating panel and is self-consistent either way.
- `onSwitch` so the host keeps its own transition. This matters: the sidebar
  navigates off vault-scoped views BEFORE activating, handles a note that
  refuses to save, and calls onVaultActivated. Switching directly would have
  dropped all of that — the guard upstream added deliberately.
- `disabled` so the trigger locks while a switch is in flight, preserving the
  behaviour the native control had.

ConsumerNavigation's tests drove the <select> directly; they now drive the
picker through two helpers, keeping every behavioural assertion (moves to
Memories, locks while switching, respects a blocked note, reports failure)
unchanged. Full frontend suite green: 130 tests.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
